## A land of castles

For centuries western Bohemia was borderland — and borders are guarded by castles. Within an hour's drive of Marienbad stand more than a dozen, proudly preserved or picturesquely ruined. We picked the seven finest: the first three offer classic guided tours, the remaining four are freely accessible ruins for romantics and walkers.

## 1. Loket — a Gothic gem above the Ohře

**By car**: 40 minutes | **Time on site**: 2–4 hours

If Bohemia held a contest for its most photogenic castle, Loket would make the final. The Gothic fortress stands on a rock embraced by a tight bend of the Ohře, with a perfectly preserved little town crouching below. Goethe celebrated his birthday here; today the castle draws visitors with its exhibitions and, in summer, open-air concerts in the riverside amphitheatre.

## 2. Bečov nad Teplou — the castle with a treasure

**By car**: 30 minutes (or by train through the Teplá valley) | **Time on site**: 2–3 hours

Two castles in one: a medieval fortress and a baroque chateau layered above the town. The star is the Reliquary of St Maurus from the early 13th century — Romanesque goldsmith work whose dramatic journey through history ended beneath the floor of the castle chapel, where detectives unearthed it in 1985. After the crown jewels, the country's most precious movable monument.

## 3. Kynžvart — Chancellor Metternich's chateau

**By car**: 15 minutes | **Time on site**: 1.5–3 hours

Closest of the seven lies the neoclassical chateau of the man who redrew the map of Europe after Napoleon's fall. Chancellor Metternich assembled here a famous cabinet of curiosities — from Egyptian mummies to Napoleon's personal effects — and a daguerreotype collection inscribed in UNESCO's Memory of the World register. The vast English park is perfect for walks with a stroller or a dog.

## 4. Gutštejn — the most romantic ruin

**By car**: 35 minutes + a short walk | **Time on site**: 1.5–3 hours

Above the quiet valley of the Hadovka stream, the massive square tower of Gutštejn rises from the woods. No admission fee, no opening hours — just silence, stonework and views. Loveliest on foot from Okrouhlé Hradiště, or as a longer circuit from the little spa town of Konstantinovy Lázně.

## 5. Přimda — Bohemia's oldest stone castle

**By car**: 45 minutes + a hill climb | **Time on site**: 1.5–2.5 hours

The Romanesque donjon from around 1121 is considered the oldest stone castle on Bohemian soil. Its ruined tower watches over a striking hill above the town of the same name, on the old trade path to Bavaria; a short, brisk climb rewards you with views deep into the Bohemian Forest and across the border.

## 6. Krasíkov (Švamberk) — the castle above the fields

**By car**: 35 minutes + a climb | **Time on site**: 1–2 hours

The ancestral seat of the lords of Švamberk crowns the free-standing Ovčí vrch hill, rising straight out of open fields — which is exactly why it offers one of the finest panoramas in western Bohemia. The castle chapel and parts of the fortifications survive; total tranquillity completes the scene, as tourist crowds have never found their way here.

## 7. Andělská Hora — a view fit for angels

**By car**: 50 minutes + a short steep climb | **Time on site**: 1–1.5 hours

A Gothic ruin on a steep rocky cone just beyond Karlovy Vary. The climb takes a quarter of an hour, but the view belongs to the unforgettable category: from the Ore Mountains across the Doupov Hills to the Slavkov Forest. An ideal stop on the way back from Karlovy Vary, beautiful even in winter.

## Before you go

Loket, Bečov and Kynžvart run classic guided tours — booking ahead is wise in season. The ruins are freely accessible year-round; just bring sturdy shoes. Find all seven castles and more with the "Castles & monasteries" filter in our [day-trips catalogue](/en/day-trips).

